course: dictionary
lesson: 5035
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word injection is a good example. «At the yearly check-up a dog gets an injection, a vaccine that teaches its body to fight dangerous illnesses.» Here an injection is medicine put into the body with a needle.
explain: The same word can mean something very different in another sentence. «The car needed a fuel injection before the long trip.» Here an injection means a liquid pushed into an engine with force. The clues are car and fuel, not doctors and needles.
explain: Words can also be used in ways that are not about needles at all. «The club got an injection of cash from its sponsor.» An injection of money means a large amount added to help something. Ask yourself: what is being added, and why? That question helps you find the meaning.
fact: An injection is when a doctor or nurse uses a needle to put medicine into your body through the skin.
fact: «A jab» (UK, Australia, New Zealand) and «a shot» (USA, Canada) are everyday words people use for an injection.
fact: Vaccinations are injections that protect you from diseases like measles.
fact: At the dentist, an injection can numb your mouth so you do not feel pain while you are being treated.
fact: Never touch a needle you find lying around; tell an adult straight away.
exercise: «At the yearly check-up, the dog had an injection to protect it from dangerous illnesses.» What is an injection in this sentence? | A bath with soap | A bowl of food | A walk around the park | *Medicine put into the body through a needle
exercise: «The car needed a fuel injection before the long trip.» What does injection mean here? | A new car colour | A sudden loud noise | *A liquid pushed into the engine with force | A needle pricking the skin for a vaccine
exercise: «The town received an injection of money to build a new park.» What does injection mean here? | A bag of sweets | *A large amount of money added to help | A sharp pain in the leg | Shots fired at a target
test: «The nurse gave Sam an injection, so his arm was sore for an hour.» Where does the injection go? | Into a shoe | *Through the skin, into the body | Into the sea | Into a glass of water
test: Dad says, «We need a big injection of energy before the match.» What does injection mean here? | A needle used at the doctor | Taking away all the food | A new pair of boots | *A large amount added to give strength
exam: «He had a numbing injection at the dentist, so his lip felt strange for hours.» What is a numbing injection? | A bag of sweets given after a visit | A loud noise made by a drill | A type of fuel for cars | *A needle of medicine that makes the mouth stop feeling pain
